    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_b46a6dc4c88e2c1ca4dee6dda223daf5e6b51adfaf44466506e88d79ae7d18f1",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The span defines Psi(n) = n product_{q | n}(1+1/q), the primorial-type cutoff M_x = product_{q <= x} q, and R(n) = Psi(n)/(n log log n) for n >= 3, then states unconditionally that R(M_x) tends to exp(gamma)/zeta(2). Since M_x is squarefree and has exactly the primes q <= x as divisors, Psi(M_x)/M_x = product_{q <= x}(1+1/q), so substitution into R turns the cutoff-prime representation into a normalized quantity with a stable displayed limit. The normalization removes the explicit n factor and scales the remaining product by log log n. The limit gives neither a convergence rate nor the side of approach, so it cannot alone justify an eventual one-sided inequality when combined with another vanishing error.",
