    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8e52174d2b0bec6816223b78ff900ff72324a07fa786bb412e5c8241998f27a8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The three units jointly identify theta as an additive compression of cutoff primes, Nicolas(x) as product_{q <= x} q/(q-1) > exp(gamma) log theta(x), and f(x) as exp(gamma) log theta(x) times the reciprocal product. Thus f is the right-to-left ratio in the Nicolas comparison, so Nicolas(x) is equivalent to f(x) < 1 wherever the displayed quantities are defined. Lemma 2.1 then uses that same normalization at a different threshold: an eventual f(x) <= exp(2/sqrt(x)) envelope conflicts with the cited arbitrarily late positive spikes of log f under false RH. This connects the exact criterion to the sufficient reductio without treating the two bounds as identical.",
